---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import MessageList from '../../components/docs/MessageList.astro';
import Code from '../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/troubleshooting/');

const probe = `curl -sv http://your-mac.local:54330/
# {"service":"hozz-receiver","ready":true,"name":"Brandon's Mac"}`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    Most of what looks like a fault here is Hozz reporting a state honestly. The states that read
    worst — waiting, retrying, no data for a type — are usually the app declining to claim something
    it cannot support. This page separates those from the genuine problems.
  </p>

  <h2>Every message, and what it means</h2>

  <p>
    These are the exact strings the app shows, so you can search the words on your screen.
  </p>

  <MessageList />

  <h2>The Mac does not appear on the phone</h2>

  <p>Work through these in order — they are ordered by how often each is the cause.</p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Is Hozz open on the Mac?</h3>
      <p>
        The receiver only runs while the app does. A Mac you have used before shows{' '}
        <strong>Offline — open Hozz on it</strong> rather than being offered, because offering a
        computer that cannot answer only produces a failure you could not have avoided.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Are both devices on the same network?</h3>
      <p>
        Guest networks, client isolation and separate 2.4/5GHz SSIDs that do not bridge will all
        prevent discovery. So will the phone being on cellular.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Does the phone have Local Network permission?</h3>
      <p>
        If it says <strong>Hozz needs permission to see this network</strong>, grant it in{' '}
        <strong>Settings → Hozz → Local Network</strong>. Without it, Bonjour cannot look.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Is the Mac reachable at all?</h3>
      <p>A <code>GET</code> to the port answers with a small JSON identification:</p>
      <Code caption="From any machine on the same network" code={probe} />
      <p>
        If that answers, the receiver is fine and the problem is discovery — add the Mac by web
        address instead. If it does not, the problem is the network or the Mac.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Give up on the network and use a folder</h3>
      <p>
        The Mac app also watches a folder. Point the phone at a synced folder and the Mac at the
        same folder, and you get everything the receiver gives you without either device accepting
        an inbound connection. See <a href="/docs/destinations/folder/">Folder</a>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Callout kind="note" title="If you are building the Mac app yourself">
    <p>
      Two macOS behaviours look exactly like network faults and are not. A sandboxed app needs{' '}
      <code>com.apple.security.network.client</code> to <em>answer</em>, not just{' '}
      <code>...network.server</code> to listen — a reply travels to the phone's ephemeral port,
      which the sandbox classifies as outbound. And macOS 15+ Local Network authorisation is keyed
      to the executable's UUID and cannot be reset, so a grant goes stale on every rebuild while
      System Settings still shows the app as allowed. Both are written up in the app repository's
      README.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The first sync looks stalled</h2>

  <p>
    It is almost certainly working. A first backfill covers years of history, and iOS grants
    background time in short windows, so days is normal.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>How to tell it apart from stuck:</strong> the count of completed types should rise over
    a day or two, and the Mac's Data tab should show types reaching further back. If neither moves
    at all across several days <em>and</em> you have opened the app in that time, treat it as stuck.
    See <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting started</a> for what to expect during a first sync.
  </p>

  <h2>An export came out empty, or smaller than expected</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the format.</strong> A <a href="/docs/formats/">GPX</a> export contains only
      workouts that have GPS. A treadmill run has no route and produces no file — that is not an
      error. If you chose GPX expecting a health export, you get an archive that looks broken.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the Health permissions.</strong> A denied type and an empty type are
      indistinguishable to Hozz, because Apple does not let an app tell them apart. Look at{' '}
      <strong>Settings → Health → Data Access &amp; Devices → Hozz</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check which types the destination is limited to.</strong> A destination can be scoped
      to a subset, and a scope you set months ago is easy to forget.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check whether the type has synced yet.</strong> On the Mac, a type the backfill has not
      reached is not the same as a type you have no data for, and the app distinguishes them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Background sync is not running on schedule</h2>

  <p>
    See <a href="/docs/background-sync/">Background sync</a>. The short checklist:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Have you force-quit Hozz? That stops background launches until you open it again.</li>
    <li>Is Low Power Mode on? It suspends background refresh.</li>
    <li>Is <strong>Settings → General → Background App Refresh</strong> on, globally and for Hozz?</li>
    <li>Is the phone locked when you expect the sync? Health cannot be read while it is.</li>
    <li>
      Is the cadence what you think? "When new data arrives" is still capped at hourly by HealthKit
      for most types.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>Opening the app starts a sync, and <strong>Sync now</strong> forces one immediately.</p>

  <h2>A destination says it needs attention</h2>

  <p>
    <code>needsAttention</code> means Hozz has stopped trying and wants you, rather than retrying on
    a timer forever. The two states are different on purpose: <code>retrying</code> resolves itself,{' '}
    <code>needsAttention</code> does not.
  </p>

  <p>
    One case is worth explaining because it looks like a bug. If a destination was written by a
    newer version of Hozz — or by one whose vocabulary later changed — this build may not recognise
    one of its settings. When that happens Hozz <strong>keeps the record and refuses to use it</strong>:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>The unrecognised value is held as-is and written back out untouched, so a build that cannot read a setting cannot erode it either.</li>
    <li>The destination stays in the list, marked as needing attention, naming which setting and which value it did not understand.</li>
    <li>Nothing is delivered to it, not even on <strong>Sync now</strong>, and a connection test refuses rather than reporting it as working.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Editing and saving is the escape hatch, and the editor says plainly that saving is also the
    moment the original setting is replaced.
  </p>

  <h2>An unfinished export cannot be continued</h2>

  <p>
    An export written by a later version whose stored words this build does not recognise is still
    shown, with the reason, and can be discarded. Hiding it would leave your export simply gone;
    offering to continue it would promise something that fails on the tap.
  </p>

  <h2>The MCP server reports no data</h2>

  <p>
    Two different problems look identical: the data directory path is wrong, or nothing has synced
    to that Mac yet. <a href="/docs/mcp/#if-it-reports-no-data">The MCP page</a> covers telling them
    apart — the short version is to copy the configuration from the Mac app's Assistant tab rather
    than typing a path, because the database lives inside the sandboxed app's container.
  </p>

  <h2>Still stuck</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz is early alpha, and some of what you hit will genuinely be a bug.{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/thatcube/hozz/issues">Open an issue</a> — logs never contain Health
    values, credentials or destination secrets, so they are safe to attach.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
